  {
    "episode_id": 92599,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92599.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "My guest today, Congressman Jamie Raskin, said something recently that has really stuck with me. He said, when we were growing up, my dad used to say to us, when everything looks hopeless, you're the hope. Jamie is someone that I, his constituents in the state of Maryland, and a lot of people in the United States looked to for hope. He studied and fell in love with the Constitution, then spent years molding young minds as a law school professor and joined Congress in 2017. But in late 2020, everything changed. It was the height of COVID. He lost his beloved 25-year-old son, Tommy. And just one week later, Jamie was in the Capitol on January 6th, while insurrectionists stormed the building. Jamie wrote beautifully, exquisitely, about these darkest of dark days in his book, Unthinkable, Trauma, Truth, and the Trials of American Democracy. And in it, he tells the story of the lifeline, the path that led him back to hope. This is Hope as a Muscle. I'm Mary Chapin Carpenter. Can you tell me how you found your way into activism in law school? What did it feel like when you first started getting engaged in that regard?",
