  {
    "episode_id": 92599,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92599.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Well, the big issue when I was in law school was the question of getting the corporations to divest from apartheid South Africa. And I've noticed with a lot of politicians my age, like Barack Obama, who I think is just like two years older than me, a lot of us were anchored in that period. Jennifer Graham Hall was his secretary of energy and was governor of Michigan, was in my class in law school. And we got in trouble together at Harvard Law School. Harvard Law School sent a letter to the bar about both of us, admonishing us and discipling us for engaging in mass rally situations to get Harvard to divest its money from apartheid. That was an issue that our generation felt very strongly about. I mean, it was a completely racist authoritarian society where black people and so-called colored people and all of the different ethnic groups were subordinated in a racial hierarchy and apartheid made life just intolerable and sufferable for people in South Africa. So anyway, that was the galvanizing question of our times. Harvard, unfortunately, did not acquit itself very well. There were a lot of universities and colleges that got out that did divest. And Harvard kept slicing the baloney really fine. Well, we'll divest from corporations that have a majority of their investments in South Africa. So we ended up having a huge demonstration with 15,000 or 20,000 people. in a Harvard Yard telling Harvard to stand in the moral center and make the right decision.",
