    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92599.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I know. I felt it very much at the time that she threw me a lifeline. I mean, I didn't know whether I would be able to grab onto it and pull myself out. I mean, I felt like I'd been traveling in the underworld and part of it was COVID-19, which was just this blanket of darkness, as you know, that fell upon the land. And for people struggling with mental health issues like Tommy was, it was a great intensification of the isolation and the depression. You know, there were record rates of mental illness and record rates of suicide and just horrific things happening. And then we lost Tommy and it felt like the whole world was turned upside down for us. And then there was January the 6th, the psychiatrist I remember reading about. define trauma as a radical violent rupture from your basic expectations of what life will be like. And we certainly experienced that trauma with the loss of Tommy, who was so much, you know, with Hannah and Tabitha, the center of our world. And we experienced it again just one week later, literally from Wednesday to Wednesday, with this attempted a political coup, an overthrow of the presidential election in a violent insurrection, the coup. And that also was a traumatizing thing because... Absolutely. You know, we always expected that there would be a peaceful transfer of power and no matter how egregious the candidate or no matter how dramatic the difference is that everybody would fundamentally accept the constitutional order.",
