    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92599.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 75,
    "turn_text": "Well, you do have to look to the past because there are people who have faced much worse odds than we have in times of much worse danger and oppression. I qualify that only by saying we haven't really gotten to confront the real danger of our times yet, which is not Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin. We're going to sweep them away. It is climate change. That's really what we have to confront. And that presents a completely different paradigm for understanding politics. And again, I'll have to come back to talk about that one. But when you look at American history, you look at what the abolitionists were facing and black slaves were facing in America, what Frederick Douglass and Harriet Tubman we're looking at. You look at the civil rights movement and you look at Bob Moses who went down to Mississippi. He was a graduate student in philosophy and math at Harvard. He opened up the newspaper. He saw black students sitting in at a lunch counter and he said they looked the way that I felt. and he knew that he had to go down to the south and he went to Mississippi and he thought he was going to be organizing sit-ins and motels and hotels and restaurants and he met Amsey Moore who was the head of the local NAACP where he went. And he said, no, we don't need that. He said, look around you. You're in a congressional district that's two thirds black, and nobody can vote. Less than 1% of African Americans were registered. Bob Moses came up with the slogan, one person, one vote. That wasn't invented by the Supreme Court. That's what he was saying, going door to door, nearly getting himself killed. but it galvanized the entire nation. It led to the Freedom Summer, the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party, the challenge of the Democratic Convention, the transformation of the Democrats, the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Voting Rights Act of 1965. We've got to make the same kinds of decisions with courage and honor that Bob Moses and Snick made in the 1960s in our own time. in terms of confronting injustice, and tyranny, and the autographs. For millions and millions of Americans of all ages, but especially young people, they've been so inspired and uplifted and galvanized,",
