  {
    "episode_id": 92599,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92599.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 81,
    "turn_text": "So I look to the young people because they're beyond racism, antisemitism, homophobia, immigrant bashing and misogyny. But I also look to history and two of the key figures in my heart, in my mind, our Frederick Douglass and Tom Payne. And Douglass was born about 45 minutes away from my house at the Y River Plantation into slavery. And he escaped from slavery and became a great freedom fighter and never gave up hope, including in the US Constitution. When other people like William Lloyd Garrison were giving up on the Constitution saying it was a pact with the devil, he was saying, no, there's very good stuff in the Constitution. We're going to improve the Constitution. And after the Civil War, we did, and we're continuing to do that. But anyway, Douglas said, if there is no struggle, there's no progress. And the struggle may be moral, it may be physical, it may be moral and physical, but there must be struggle. Power concedes nothing without a demand. It never has, and it never will. And then Tom Payne, who our beloved Tommy was named after, came to America in 1774, two years before the American Revolution. He fell in love with the promise of America. He said, if this land lives up to its values and its ideals, it will become an asylum to humanity, he said. Not an insane asylum, mind you, but a place of refuge for people seeking freedom from political and religious and economic oppression. And then he wrote common sense. about the sense we have in common and we're willing to reason and listen to each other and speak together without, you know, religious dictatorship and without conspiracy theory and all that. And it incited the American Revolution. And it gave hope to people. But 1776 was a tough year. And half of the people were running around saying, for all of human history, people have lived with kings and queens and zars and emperors and merger of church and state and compulsory religion. And you can't break that. And the other half of the people were willing to gamble on it. But people would get nervous and people would get anxious. And they saw that. And so just like now, he split right down the middle and pain wanted to give people hope and encouragement. So he wrote this pamphlet called the American Crisis. I just want to quote this little passage for you, Chapin. And I'm going to update the language pursuant to instructions from Nancy Pelosi because she said pain was a feminist and now he was. And so he wouldn't mind. And I don't want to offend modern sensibilities. And so anyway, pain wrote this. He said, these are the times that try men and women's souls. the summer soldier and the sunshine patriot will shrink at this moment from the service of their cause in their country. But everyone who stands with us now will win the love and the favor and the affection of every man and every woman for all time tyranny like hell is not easily conquered. But we have this saving consolation. The more difficult the struggle, the more glorious in the end will be our victory. So let's make that victory. Ours! This year, Mary Chapin Carpeter, we can do it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
